\par }\pard \ltrpar\qc \li0\ri0\sa160\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid6891856 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af37\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs32\insrsid5842056\charrsid6891856 Bright Children
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid15336038 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\insrsid5842056\charrsid6891856 A.   By the time Laszlo Polgar\rquote 
s first baby was born in 1969 he already had firm views on child-rearing. An eccentric citizen of communist Hungary, he had written a book called \'93Bring up Genius!\'94 and one of his favourite sayings was \'93Geniuses are made, not born\'94
. An expert on the theory of chess, he proceeded to teach little Zsuzsa at home, spending up to ten hours a day on the game. Two more da
ughters were similarly hot-housed. All three obliged their father by becoming world-class players. The youngest, Judit, is currently ranked 13th in the world, and is by far the best female chess player of all time. Would the experiment have succeeded with
 a different trio of children? If any child can be turned into a star, then a lot of time and money are being wasted worldwide on trying to pick winners.
\par B.   America has long held \'93talent searches\'94, using test results and teacher recommendations to select 
children for advanced school courses, summer schools and other extra tuition. This provision is set to grow. In his state-of-the-union address in 2006, President George Bush announced the \'93American Competitiveness Initiative\'94
, which, among much else, would train 70,000 high-school teachers to lead advanced courses for selected pupils in mathematics and science. Just as the superpowers\rquote 
 space race made Congress put money into science education, the thought of China and India turning out hundreds of thousands of engineers and scientists is scaring America into prodding its brightest to do their best.
\par C.   The philosophy behind this talent search is that ability is innate; that it can be diagnosed with considerable accuracy; and that it is worth cultivating. In America, bright children are ranked as \'93moderately\'94, \'93highly\'94, \'93exceptionally
\'94 and \'93profoundly\'94 gifted. The only chance to influence innate ability is thought to be in the womb or the first couple of years of life. Hence the fad for \'93teaching aids\'94 such as videos and flashcards for newborns, and \'93whale sounds\'94
 on tape which a pregnant mother can strap to her belly.
\par D.   In Britain, there is a broadly similar belief in the existence of innate talent, but also an egalitarian sentiment which makes people quea
sy about the idea of investing resources in grooming intelligence. Teachers are often opposed to separate provision for the best-performing children, saying any extra help should go to stragglers. In 2002, in a bid to help the able while leaving intact th
e
 ban on most selection by ability in state schools, the government set up the National Academy for Gifted and Talented Youth. This outfit runs summer schools and master classes for children nominated by their schools. To date, though, only seven in ten se
condary schools have nominated even a single child. Last year all schools were told they must supply the names of their top 10%.
\par E.   Picking winners is also the order of the day in ex-communist states, a hangover from the times when talented individuals we
re plucked from their homes and ruthlessly trained for the glory of the nation. But in many other countries, opposition to the idea of singling out talent and grooming it runs deep. In Scandinavia, a belief in virtues like modesty and social solidarity ma
kes people flinch from the idea of treating brainy children differently.
\par F.   And in Japan, there is a widespread belief that all children are born with the same innate abilities \endash  and should, therefore, be treated alike. All are taught together, covering t
he same syllabus at the same rate until they finish compulsory schooling. Those who learn quickest are expected then to teach their classmates. In China, extra teaching is provided, but to a self-selected bunch. \'93Children\rquote s palaces\'94
 in big cities offer a huge range of after-school classes. Anyone can sign up; all that is asked is excellent attendance.
\par G.   Statistics give little clue as to which system is best. The performance of the most able is heavily affected by factors other than state provision. Most 
state education in Britain is nominally non-selective, but middle-class parents try to live near the best schools. Ambitious Japanese parents have made private, out-of-school tuition a thriving business. And Scandinavia\rquote 
s egalitarianism might work less well in places with more diverse populations and less competent teachers. For what it\rquote s worth, the data suggest that some countries \endash  like Japan and Finland, see table \endash 
 can eschew selection and still thrive. But that does not mean that any country can ditch selection and do as well.
\par H.   Mr Polgar thought any child could be a prodigy given the right teaching, an early start and enough practice. At one point he planned to prove it by adopting three baby boys from a poor country and trying his methods on them. (His wife vetoed the sche
m
e.) Some say the key to success is simply hard graft. Judit, the youngest of the Polgar sisters, was the most driven, and the most successful; Zsofia, the middle one, was regarded as the most talented, but she was the only one who did not achieve the stat
us of grandmaster. \'93Everything came easiest to her,\'94 said her older sister. \'93But she was lazy.\'94
